# Downloaded source data + generated outputs
data/*
*.csv

# Every folder carries a README.md explaining what is in it (added 2026-08-08); those must
# survive a fresh clone. data/ and outputs/ are the only two folders whose contents are
# ignored wholesale, so they are the only two whose README needs an explicit un-ignore
# (outputs/ has its own, next to the outputs/* rule below). Every other folder is tracked
# normally and its README needs no rule at all.
!data/README.md

# ...but DO commit the small illustrative result samples
!data_samples/
!data_samples/*.csv

# ...and DO commit the static monthly macro series (fixed historical record, pulled once).
# Note: ignore data/* (contents) not data/ (dir) so this un-ignore can take effect.
!data/macro_monthly.csv

# ...and the generated feature-importance tables (small, handy to diff across runs).
# Moved beside their models 2026-08-02, so un-ignore the new paths too.
!feature_importance.csv
!models/poll/*.csv
!models/fundamentals/*.csv

# ...and DO commit the FROZEN historical poll snapshots (Internet Archive captures of 538,
# which never change). Committing them removes the fragile/rate-limited live Wayback dependency.
!data/senate_historical.csv
!data/house_historical.csv
!data/governor_historical.csv
!data/generic_ballot_historical.csv

# ...and the NYT "current" poll files — the 2024 cycle is OVER, so these are frozen history now
# too. If re-fetched, the NYT endpoint would serve 2026-cycle polls (or nothing) and silently
# change the training data. Committed = the training set is reproducible offline.
!data/senate_current.csv
!data/house_current.csv
!data/governor_current.csv

# ...and the results + metadata files (538's repos are unmaintained; past results never change;
# the repos could vanish). With these, ALL model inputs are committed — no network on any run.
!data/res_senate.csv
!data/res_house.csv
!data/res_governor.csv
!data/races.csv
!data/lean_states.csv
!data/lean_districts.csv
!data/raw_polls_538.csv
!data/generic_ballot_hist_538.csv
!data/approval_monthly.csv
!data/generic_ballot_monthly.csv
!data/fec_summary.csv
!data/fec_detail.csv
!data/governor_finance.csv
!data/district_pvi_current.csv
!data/redistricted_2026.csv
!data/candidate_party_overrides.csv
!data/dropped_out_2026.csv
# Roster/name-integrity inputs added 2026-08-05/06. These MUST be committed: CI checks out
# this repo fresh, so anything left ignored simply does not exist there and the filters that
# read it become silent no-ops. That is exactly what happened - drop_primary_losers() found
# no primary_results file in CI, so the nightly Model refresh regenerated predictions with
# Haley Stevens and Perry Johnson (both primary LOSERS) back in their general-election races,
# overwriting the local fix twice before anyone noticed.
!data/primary_results_2026.csv
# The deep Senate/Governor archive (1998-2024, added 2026-08-07). load_primary_results()
# reads it, so leaving it ignored makes primary_margin silently sparser in CI than locally -
# the same shape as the bug that reverted the MI-Sen roster fix twice.
!data/primary_results_deep_hist.csv
!data/name_aliases.csv
!data/primary_dates_2026_manual.csv

# ...and the trained production model artifacts (predict.py / predict_margin.py load these)
!data/model_xgb.json
!data/model_features.json
!data/margin_model_xgb.json
!data/margin_model_features.json

# ...and the margin model's importance table (small, diffable, like feature_importance.csv)
!margin_feature_importance.csv

# transient scraper run-logs (progress traces via `tee`; not source data — the CSVs are).
# Consolidated into logs/scrape/ on 2026-08-08. Before that, 20 of these sat loose in the
# repo root and the three name-specific patterns below covered only 10 of them, so the other
# 10 (candidate_bios_rescrape_log*, house_*_scrape_log*, house_primary_scrape_log) were
# COMMITTED while their identical-in-kind siblings were ignored. One directory rule now
# covers every scraper log regardless of what a future scraper names its output.
logs/*
!logs/README.md
# Keep the READMEs. Note the middle rule: git never looks INSIDE an excluded directory, so
# `!logs/scrape/README.md` alone does nothing - logs/scrape must be re-included first, and
# its contents re-excluded, before the README can be un-ignored.
!logs/scrape/
logs/scrape/*
!logs/scrape/README.md
# legacy root-level patterns, kept so an old checkout or a re-run from the root still ignores
ballotpedia_scrape*.txt
backfill_*_scrape_log.txt
backfill_*_log.txt

# Generated prediction artifacts (moved out of the repo root into outputs/ on 2026-08-08).
# The CSVs are already covered by the blanket `*.csv` rule above; the meta sidecars and the
# SHAP explanation JSONs are named explicitly so the intent is on the record rather than
# depending on which rule happens to fire first.
#
# The two explanation JSONs WERE committed while they sat in the root - not by decision, but
# because the root `*.csv` rule does not cover .json. They are regenerated by every
# refresh_dashboard run and are copied into polling-agg (which commits its own copy under
# data/processed/, and that is the one the dashboard actually serves). Keeping a second
# committed copy here only produced merge conflicts on a file nothing reads from this side.
outputs/*
!outputs/README.md
# (ignore outputs/* not outputs/, or the un-ignore above cannot take effect - same idiom as
# data/* and logs/*)

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.env

# PRIMARY model static inputs + artifacts (2026-07-15): Wikipedia-scraped training polls
# (pages get edited — a re-scrape is NOT reproducible), hand-entered dates, built dataset,
# trained artifact. Same static-data principle as above.
!data/primary_polls_wikipedia.csv
!data/primary_polls_long.csv
!data/primary_dates_hist.csv
!data/primary_model_xgb.json
!data/primary_model_features.json

# PRIMARY MARGIN model (models/poll/primary_margin_model.py, added 2026-08-02)
!data/primary_margin_model_xgb.json
!data/primary_margin_model_features.json

# Fundamentals-only (no race polling) reference models - fundamentals_model.py. Same
# static-artifact principle as the production models above: small, deterministic from
# committed data, and the eval numbers in the _features sidecar are the record of what a
# poll-free prior can actually do. NOT wired into predict.py or the dashboard.
!data/fundamentals_model_general.json
!data/fundamentals_model_general_features.json
!data/fundamentals_model_primary.json
!data/fundamentals_model_primary_features.json

# Senate/Governor primary RESULTS (fetch_primary_results_2026.py --hist) - was never
# actually un-ignored despite being static-data-principle scraped output; found + fixed
# 2026-07-22 while building the House equivalent below (it was living local-only).
!data/primary_results_hist.csv

# House primary RESULTS, 1998-2024 (fetch_house_primary_results_hist.py, 2026-07-22):
# Wikipedia per-state "House elections in X" summary pages - not reproducible on re-scrape
# (pages get edited), fact-checked (see that script's docstring), committed once.
!data/house_primary_results_hist.csv

# committed 2026-07-16 for the model-refresh GitHub Action: CI clones this repo fresh and
# predict*.py needs the historical polls (house effects + bias priors) without running
# build_dataset.ipynb. 14MB, regenerable, but committing = static-data principle anyway.
!polls_long_with_results.csv
!data/candidate_bios.csv

# per-office bio SOURCE files (2026-07-24 save-discipline change: each office scrapes to
# its own file, combine_candidate_bios.py builds the merged candidate_bios.csv above -
# commit the sources too or the layout only exists on one machine)
!data/candidate_bios_senate.csv
!data/candidate_bios_governor.csv
!data/candidate_bios_house.csv
# Ballotpedia gap-filler (person-level; combine_candidate_bios.py expands + merges it,
# Wikipedia preferred) + the uncovered-candidates list it targets
!data/candidate_bios_ballotpedia.csv
!data/uncovered_candidates.csv
# the still-uncovered office_level target roster (regenerated after each backfill stage;
# drives the Ballotpedia/manual passes) — small, diffable, reference-able
!data/office_level_backfill_targets.csv
# Stage-3 HAND-CODED office histories (offices_json tenure dates + source_note) for winners
# no scraper could reach - static, reviewable, the authoritative manual source for the builder
!data/candidate_bios_manual.csv

# FROZEN 2026-cycle dataset (freeze_2026_dataset.py, 2026-07-29): a self-contained record of
# the cycle - raw polls (append-ready to the training set once results land), the built feature
# table (the exact model input), meta/provenance, and DATED prediction snapshots (point-in-time
# forecasts; the LIVE predictions_2026.csv stays CI-regenerated + gitignored). Same static-data
# principle as the frozen historical snapshots.
!data/polls_2026_long.csv
!data/candidate_table_2026.csv
!data/dataset_2026_meta.json
!data/predictions_2026_snapshot_*.csv
!data/margin_predictions_2026_snapshot_*.csv

# local archive of pre-change data copies (archive-don't-delete rule is a LOCAL backup
# discipline — these are point-in-time snapshots, not repo content; never committed, same
# as the earlier archive/candidate_bios_<timestamp> files documented in METHODOLOGY).
# Ignore archive/* not archive/, so the README un-ignore below can take effect: git never
# looks inside an excluded DIRECTORY. Same idiom as data/*, outputs/* and logs/*.
archive/*
!archive/README.md
